{
  "purpose": "results/affordance.md scores the black-box tier as 'works, IF you can name candidate principals', and never measures how bad that condition is. The organisers' own framing is that it is survivable because principals are a tractable list of a few dozen. This tests that premise directly. A suspect model carries a loyalty to one entity; the auditor has a candidate list of K entities containing it and no activation access. For each candidate the auditor measures order-cancelled favouring against fixed reference rivals and names the argmax. Reported as top-1 identification accuracy versus K, exactly, over three known-loyal organisms. The load-bearing control is the same procedure run on the NEUTRAL condition, where the answer must be chance: without it a high accuracy could be a property of the entity names rather than of the loyalty.",
